Now I have the PLL bit field definitions:
- PLL_EN_BIT (16) — matches lvds.c PLL_ENABLE BIT(16) ✓
- PLL_OUT_SYS (18) — matches lvds.c PLL_OUT_SYS BIT(18) ✓
- PLL_OUT_MUX (20) — matches lvds.c PLL_OUT_MUX BIT(20) ✓
- PLL_FACTORM_EN_BIT (19) — matches lvds.c PLL_FACTOR_M_EN BIT(19) ✓
- PLL_FACTORN_BIT (8), PLL_FACTORN_MASK (0xff)
- PLL_FACTORM_BIT (4), PLL_FACTORM_MASK (0x3)
- PLL_FACTORP_BIT (0), PLL_FACTORP_MASK (0x1)
- PLL_SDM_AMP_BIT (0), PLL_SDM_FREQ_BIT (17), PLL_SDM_STEP_BIT (20), PLL_SDM_MODE_BIT (29), PLL_SDM_EN_BIT (31)

Wait — PLL_FRAC_EN_BIT (20) also. For FRA type, bit 20 = FRAC_EN. For SDM type... PLL_OUT_MUX is bit 20 as well? Both are bit 20. Hmm. Interesting - In D13x, PLL_OUT_MUX is at bit 20 for GEN register, and PLL_FRAC_EN_BIT (20) for FRA register. Wait, FRA_EN is in the FRA register (offset_fra = PLL_FRA2_CFG_REG? Actually offset_fra = cfg reg). Actually in clk_pll_recalc_rate: `fra_en = (readl(cmu_reg(pll->offset_fra)) >> PLL_FRAC_EN_BIT) & 0x1;`. And offset_fra is the CFG register. Hmm, the PLL_FRA type uses offset_fra = PLL_FRA2_CFG_REG (0x0068) per the PLL_FRA() macro definition. Let me check the PLL_FRA macro.

Anyway, the lvds.c code:
```
reg = getreg32(CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);
reg &= ~PLL_OUT_MUX;
putreg32(reg, CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);

reg &= ~(0xffffu | PLL_FACTOR_M_EN | (0x1fu << 24));
reg |= PLL_FACTOR_M_EN | (120u << 8) | (3u << 4) | 1u;
putreg32(reg, CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);
putreg32(BIT(31) | (2u << 29) | (360u << 20) | (3u << 17), CMU_PLL_FRA2_SDM);

reg = getreg32(CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);
reg |= PLL_ENABLE | PLL_OUT_SYS;
putreg32(reg, CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);
up_udelay(200);
reg = getreg32(CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);
reg |= PLL_OUT_MUX;
putreg32(reg, CMU_PLL_FRA2_GEN);
```

Now compare to the vendor algorithm:
- factor_n=120 (N=120), factor_m=3 (M=3), factor_p=1 (P=1). So GEN: `PLL_FACTORM_EN(1<<19) | factor_n(120<<8) | factor_m(3<<4) | factor_p(1<<0)`.
  - Wait: `reg |= PLL_FACTOR_M_EN | (120u << 8) | (3u << 4) | 1u;` → 120<<8 = 0x7800, 3<<4 = 0x30, 1. This is N=120, M=3, P=1. OK matches.
  - But note `reg &= ~(0xffffu | PLL_FACTOR_M_EN | (0x1fu << 24));`. 0xffff covers bits 0-15, which includes P (bit0), M (bits4-5), N (bits8-15). Good. `(0x1fu << 24)` covers bits 24-28 (DITHER/ICP bits). For SDM, the vendor code does `reg_val &= ~(0x1F << 24)` (set PLL_ICP=0). OK.
- SDM register: `BIT(31) | (2u << 29) | (360u << 20) | (3u << 17)`.
  - BIT(31) = SDM_EN ✓
  - 2<<29 = SDM_MODE=2 ✓ (triangular)
  - 360<<20 = SDM_STEP=360 ✓
  - 3<<17 = SDM_FREQ=3 ✓
  - But AMP not set (bits 0-16=0) → sdm_amp=0 ✓ matches "SDM amplitude 0"
- Then enable PLL_ENABLE|PLL_OUT_SYS, delay 200us, set PLL_OUT_MUX (bypass off).

Wait — the vendor clk_pll_set_rate sets PLL_OUT_MUX only after lock wait. Actually vendor sequence: bypass PLL (clear OUT_MUX) → configure GEN → configure SDM → set PLL_OUT_SYS|PLL_EN → wait 200us → clk_pll_bypass(0) (set OUT_MUX). The lvds.c does exactly this. Good.

Note: PLL_FACTORN_MIN = 14, MAX = 199. N=120 OK.

Now PLL output freq: rate = parent/2 * (120+1)/(3+1) = 24/2 * 121/4 = 12*30.25 = 363 MHz. Hmm, 364 MHz claimed. Let me compute: parent_rate = 24MHz OSC. factor_p=1, so parent/(p+1) = 12MHz. (n+1)=121, (m+1)=4. 12*121/4 = 363 MHz. But SDM mode, so rate is slightly modulated by SDM, but base = 363MHz. The comment says "364 MHz LVDS serial clock". Slightly off but within SDM.

Then SCLK = FRA2/1, PIX = SCLK/7 → 363/7 = 51.86 MHz ≈ 52MHz. OK.

Now the DISP register config:
```
reg = getreg32(CMU_CLK_DISP);
reg &= ~((0x7u << 0) | (0x1fu << 4) | (0x3u << 10) | (0x3u << 12));
reg |= 6u << 4;
putreg32(reg, CMU_CLK_DISP);
```

Per DISPCLK macro: `DISPCLK(CLK_PIX, "pixclk", CLK_SCLK, PARENT("sclk"), CLK_DISP_REG, 0, 0, 4, 5, ...)` and `DISPCLK(CLK_SCLK, "sclk", CLK_PLL_FRA2, PARENT("pll_fra2"), CLK_DISP_REG, 0, 3, ...)`. 

Let me check the DISPCLK macro definition to know the field layout. Let me look at aic_hal_disp_clk.c.

